From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] configfs: key the format table, do not assume walk order

The header-based discovery in the previous patch made uvc-gadget die on a
SIGSEGV with no message at all:

    piwebcam[196]: CONFIGFS: Initial path: /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget
    systemd[1]: uvc-webcam.service: Main process exited, code=killed, status=11/SEGV

and, because f_uvc sets bind_deactivated, that took the whole gadget with it -
no camera, no console, no control port.

The fault is in this file, not in the header walk. configfs_fill_formats()
decided whether a file belonged to a new table entry by comparing it against
the LAST entry allocated. That held only because ftw() visited every attribute
of one frame consecutively, for a single USB speed. Header-based discovery
registers each file for fs, hs AND ss in turn, so consecutive calls alternate
speed, the adjacency test never matches, and it allocated a fresh entry per
attribute per speed: about 120 where 15 were wanted.

uvc_frame_format[] holds 30. The bounds check ran on the INCREMENT only:

    if (uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].defined) {
        last_format_index++;
        if (last_format_index > 29) {
            goto free;              /* leaves the counter at 30 */
        }
    }
    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].usb_speed = usb_speed;   /* writes [30] */

so on the next call [30] read back undefined, the increment was skipped, and
the write went ahead one past the end. It then walked further out on each
call, scribbling structs through .bss until something the process needed was
gone. That is why the crash landed after the walk rather than at the overflow,
and why it left nothing in the log.

Matching on the key - speed, format, frame name - is order-independent, which
is all ftw() actually guarantees, and the write is now bounded by the array's
own size rather than a literal.

Two things fall out of the same walk-order assumption:

  - bFormatIndex lives one level above the frames it applies to, so readdir
    can hand it over before them, and set_uvc_format_index() only stamps
    entries that already exist. On that ordering the value was dropped and the
    table ended up in walk order - which is precisely the bug where a host
    asking for H.264 was handed MJPEG. It is now remembered and applied at
    entry creation as well.

  - dwFrameInterval is a list, one interval per line, and nothing consumes it
    (set_uvc_format_value knows dwDefaultFrameInterval, not this one).
    configfs_read_value() caps reads at 10 bytes, so it reported -ENODATA once
    per frame per speed - 15 lines of failure in the boot report for a file
    that was never wanted. Skipped by name instead.

Measured before this patch: 15 "unreadable value (-61) for */dwFrameInterval"
lines, then SIGSEGV.
---
--- a/uvc-gadget.c
+++ b/uvc-gadget.c
@@ -2107,9 +2107,114 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/* DashBerry: bFormatIndex arrives out of band.
+ *
+ * It lives one level above the frames it applies to
+ * (streaming/header/<h>/<fmt>/bFormatIndex), and ftw() hands out a directory's
+ * entries in readdir order, so it can be seen either before or after the frame
+ * directories under it. set_uvc_format_index() only stamps entries that
+ * ALREADY exist, so on the before-ordering the value is silently dropped - and
+ * a table with no bFormatIndex is built in walk order, which is exactly the
+ * bug where a host asking for H.264 was handed MJPEG.
+ *
+ * Remembering it makes the walk order irrelevant: entries created later are
+ * stamped at creation, entries created earlier are stamped as before.
+ */
+static struct {
+    enum usb_device_speed usb_speed;
+    int video_format;
+    unsigned int bFormatIndex;
+    bool seen;
+} dashberry_format_index[16];
+
+static void remember_format_index(enum usb_device_speed usb_speed, int video_format,
+    unsigned int bFormatIndex)
+{
+    unsigned int i;
+
+    set_uvc_format_index(usb_speed, video_format, bFormatIndex);
+
+    for (i = 0; i < sizeof(dashberry_format_index) / sizeof(dashberry_format_index[0]); i++) {
+        if (!dashberry_format_index[i].seen ||
+            (dashberry_format_index[i].usb_speed == usb_speed &&
+             dashberry_format_index[i].video_format == video_format)) {
+            dashberry_format_index[i].usb_speed = usb_speed;
+            dashberry_format_index[i].video_format = video_format;
+            dashberry_format_index[i].bFormatIndex = bFormatIndex;
+            dashberry_format_index[i].seen = true;
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+static unsigned int recall_format_index(enum usb_device_speed usb_speed, int video_format)
+{
+    unsigned int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < sizeof(dashberry_format_index) / sizeof(dashberry_format_index[0]); i++) {
+        if (dashberry_format_index[i].seen &&
+            dashberry_format_index[i].usb_speed == usb_speed &&
+            dashberry_format_index[i].video_format == video_format) {
+            return dashberry_format_index[i].bFormatIndex;
+        }
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
+/* DashBerry: find - or create - the table entry for one speed+format+frame.
+ *
+ * This used to be "is it the same as the LAST entry?", which held only because
+ * ftw() visited every attribute of a frame consecutively, for a single speed.
+ * Header-based discovery (below) registers each file for fs, hs AND ss in
+ * turn, so consecutive calls alternate speed and the adjacency test never
+ * matches: it allocated a fresh entry per attribute per speed - about 120
+ * where 15 were wanted - walked off the end of a 30-entry array and, because
+ * the bounds check ran only on the increment and not on the write, scribbled
+ * structs through .bss until the process died on a SIGSEGV with no message.
+ *
+ * Matching on the key itself is order-independent, which is all the walk
+ * actually guarantees. The table needs 3 speeds x 2 formats x 5 frames = 15.
+ */
+static int uvc_format_slot(enum usb_device_speed usb_speed, int video_format,
+    const char * format_name)
+{
+    static bool warned = false;
+    const int capacity = (int)(sizeof(uvc_frame_format) / sizeof(uvc_frame_format[0]));
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i <= last_format_index && i < capacity; i++) {
+        if (uvc_frame_format[i].defined &&
+            uvc_frame_format[i].usb_speed == usb_speed &&
+            uvc_frame_format[i].video_format == video_format &&
+            !strcmp(uvc_frame_format[i].format_name, format_name)) {
+            return i;
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].defined) {
+        if (last_format_index + 1 >= capacity) {
+            if (!warned) {
+                printf("CONFIGFS: format table full at %d entries - dropping %s\n",
+                       capacity, format_name);
+                warned = true;
+            }
+            return -1;
+        }
+        last_format_index++;
+    }
+
+    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].usb_speed = usb_speed;
+    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].video_format = video_format;
+    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].format_name = strdup(format_name);
+    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].bFormatIndex = recall_format_index(usb_speed, video_format);
+    uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].defined = true;
+    return last_format_index;
+}
+
 static void configfs_fill_formats(const char * path, const char * part)
 {
     int index = 0;
+    int slot;
     int value = 0;
     enum usb_device_speed usb_speed;
     int video_format;
@@ -2139,6 +2244,15 @@
             goto free;
         }
 
+        /* dwFrameInterval is a LIST - one interval per line - and nothing here
+         * consumes it: set_uvc_format_value knows dwDefaultFrameInterval, not
+         * this one. configfs_read_value() rejects anything over 10 bytes, so
+         * it reported -ENODATA once per frame per speed and put 15 lines of
+         * failure in the log for a file that was never wanted. */
+        if (!strncmp(array[index - 1], "dwFrameInterval", 15)) {
+            goto free;
+        }
+
         value = configfs_read_value(path);
         if (value < 0) {
             printf("CONFIGFS: unreadable value (%d) for %s\n", value, part);
@@ -2146,7 +2260,7 @@
         }
 
         if (!strncmp(array[index - 1], "bFormatIndex", 12)) {
-            set_uvc_format_index(usb_speed, video_format, value);
+            remember_format_index(usb_speed, video_format, value);
             goto free;
         }
 
@@ -2155,27 +2269,12 @@
             goto free;
         }
 
-        if (
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].usb_speed != usb_speed ||
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].video_format != video_format ||
-            strncmp(uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].format_name, format_name, strlen(format_name))
-        ) {
-            if (uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].defined) {
-                last_format_index++;
-                
-                /* too much defined formats */
-                if (last_format_index > 29) {
-                    goto free;
-                }
-            }
-
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].usb_speed = usb_speed;
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].video_format = video_format;
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].format_name = strdup(format_name);
-            uvc_frame_format[last_format_index].defined = true;
+        slot = uvc_format_slot(usb_speed, video_format, format_name);
+        if (slot < 0) {
+            goto free;
         }
 
-        set_uvc_format_value(array[index - 1], last_format_index, value);
+        set_uvc_format_value(array[index - 1], slot, value);
     }
 
 free:
